// Lore for the Ulgu map (src/data/maps/ulgu.js). See src/data/lore/index.js.
//
// Ulgu is the realm the published cartography barely covers: one plate, the
// Shadrac Convergence, and a few hundred names on it. Where Games Workshop
// has written something — Hagg Nar, Misthávn, Greater Ulguroth, the Umbral
// Veil — these entries say it. Where it has not, they say what the plate
// itself shows: which way the mist runs, what the place is downwind of, and
// why an army would want to be standing on it. Nothing here invents a
// character, a battle or a date that the map does not already name.
//
// One thing holds across all of it. In Ulgu distance is a matter of opinion.
// A road that took a day last week takes three this week, and the realm does
// not explain itself. Every campaign fought here is really a campaign fought
// over the few places where the mist behaves.
export const ULGU_LORE = {
  // --- Fuarthorn, the northern dominion --------------------------------------
  'draichi-ganeth': `A Daughters of Khaine temple in the far north of Fuarthorn, and the sect that gives the wider order its bladed dancers. Draichi Ganeth teaches the knife over the cauldron: its Witch Aelves are drilled as a fighting school rather than a congregation, and their sisters elsewhere treat a Draichi Ganeth veteran as a loan of veteran infantry rather than a guest.

It sits at the cold end of the realm, a long way from Hagg Nar and further still from anything worth raiding, which is exactly why the temple has survived as long as it has. An army that wants Fuarthorn’s spires has to cross the whole dominion to reach them, in mist that has been taught to mislead.`,

  'darkening-vale': `A notch in Fuarthorn’s northern shelf where the realm’s ambient gloom thickens into something closer to solid. The plate marks it with a warding rune rather than a settlement, which on this map is shorthand for a place the cartographer wanted flagged and did not want to describe.

Nobody holds the Vale. Armies use it, because a force that enters at one end and is not seen again for a week is a force that has not been counted.`,

  'cursed-haunt': `A ruin on Fuarthorn’s western coast, marked and named and otherwise left alone. Whatever stood here was worth putting on a chart; whoever charted it did not go in. It matters for the anchorage rather than the ruin. A fleet working the northern shore has nowhere else to shelter for a hundred miles in either direction, and everyone who uses it leaves before dark.`,

  'the-khornescar': `A long red wound across eastern Fuarthorn, named for the god whose followers made it. Blood Legion warbands came through this ground in strength and the shadow never closed over the damage; the scar is still legible from the air, which in Ulgu is remarkable in itself.

It is the one landmark in the north that a compass cannot argue with. Campaigns in Fuarthorn tend to orient themselves off the Khornescar for exactly that reason, which makes it worth denying to the other side.`,

  fuarthorn: `The northernmost of Ulgu’s great dominions, a cold sprawl of shadow-moor and drowned coast that the realm’s politics have mostly forgotten. Its lords answer to the courts further south in the loosest possible sense, and send tithes late.

That obscurity is Fuarthorn’s whole strategic value. It is where a force musters when it does not want the Shadrac Convergence to know it is mustering, and half the campaigns that end in the Umbral Veil begin with somebody quietly moving north.`,

  'greenskul-cliffs': `A run of sea cliffs on Fuarthorn’s south shore, stained by whatever the mist deposits on them and named for it. Ships stand well off; the cliffs are the reason the northern coast has so few usable landings. For an army the cliffs are a wall with two gates in it. Hold those and Fuarthorn cannot be reinforced from the Penumbral Sea.`,

  'illican-curl': `A hook of land and the small port tucked inside it, on the northern edge of the Penumbral Sea. The Curl is a natural harbour that the mist happens to spare, which in this realm counts as a resource.

Its trade is other people’s cargo, arriving under its own arrangements and leaving under different ones. Every fleet working the northern shore calls here, and none of them ask the Curl to pick a side.`,

  // --- The Penumbral Sea ----------------------------------------------------
  witchwhorl: `A standing spiral in the Penumbral Sea’s western reach, where shadow-magic and current turn on the same axis and do not stop. Wizards come to draw on it; most of what is written about Ulgu’s weather was worked out by someone sitting at the edge of the Witchwhorl taking notes.

It is also a hazard. A fleet that crosses the whorl scatters, and a fleet that goes around it loses four days, which is long enough to lose a campaign.`,

  'grey-slash': `A knife-thin island chain lying east to west across the Penumbral Sea, too narrow to settle and too long to sail around in a hurry. The Slash divides the western sea into two weather systems that do not share news. Whoever holds its few landings controls how quickly anything crosses the north. That is the entire argument about it.`,

  onechance: `A single town on a single rock in open water, with the name it earned. Ships that miss the Onechance anchorage on the first pass do not usually get a second; the mist closes and the rock is gone. The town survives by being useful to everybody and loyal to nobody, selling water, pilotage and silence at prices that reflect the alternative.`,

  'mindrazor-coast': `The southern shore of the Penumbral Sea, where the mist comes ashore carrying something that does not agree with a mortal mind. Crews put in here for as short a time as they can manage and leave with fewer people than they landed with.

It is nonetheless the only coast on this side of the realm with beaches an army can use. Every invasion of Zoktai from the north has come over the Mindrazor, and every one of them has paid for it before the fighting started.`,

  'barbed-promise': `A small port on the sea’s southern edge with a reputation it advertises rather than denies. The Promise takes contracts, honours them to the letter, and leaves the interpretation to the client.

Mercenary companies working the Convergence hire out of here because it is the one place on the northern shore where a written agreement means something. What it means is often not what the signatory expected.`,

  'penumbral-sea': `The great pale water of Ulgu’s north, a sea of half-light where the shadow thins enough for a sail to be seen at distance — which is to say, the only place in the realm where naval warfare works the way it does anywhere else.

Everything that reaches Fuarthorn or the northern Convergence crosses the Penumbral. Its floating city, Misthávn, moors at the southern end of it, and the armadas that make up that city treat the whole sea as their own approach road.`,

  'shadewhale-coast': `The sea’s north-eastern shore, named for the enormous shadow-things that beach themselves along it. The carcasses are harvested for oil, bone and stranger materials, and the trade is worth enough to keep a coast that nobody would otherwise live on populated. A campaign that shuts the Shadewhale harvest down starves several of the northern ports of their only export inside a season.`,

  'fakers-isles': `A scatter of islands in the middle of the Penumbral Sea that are not reliably in the same place twice. Charts of the Faker’s Isles disagree with each other and with themselves, and the pilots who work them charge accordingly. Fleets hide here. The realm’s shadow does the concealing for free, and the price of finding somebody in the Isles is usually higher than the price of letting them go.`,

  // --- The Umbral Veil ------------------------------------------------------
  burntspike: `A blackened tooth of rock at the Umbral Veil’s northern gate, scorched at some point by something that did not care to be recorded. It is visible from a long way off, which makes it the Veil’s only honest landmark. Fleets running into the Veil take their bearing from Burntspike and then stop trusting bearings entirely.`,

  'the-cruel-splinters': `An archipelago of narrow stone shards guarding the north-western approach to the Umbral Veil, arranged so that there is always a channel and never the same one. Ships are lost here in numbers that the Convergence’s ports have stopped bothering to record.

For a defender the Splinters are worth a fleet. Nothing large gets through them without a pilot, and pilots can be bought, drowned or simply told the wrong thing.`,

  'umbral-reach': `The Umbral Veil is the darkest region of Ulgu, and Ulgu is the Realm of Shadow — the Veil is where the realm stops being merely dim and starts actively lying. Distance folds here. A march of a day can take three, or three can take one, and neither the marchers nor anyone waiting for them finds out which until it is over.

Morathi chose this ground deliberately. Hagg Nar sits at the Veil’s heart because an enemy who cannot be sure how far away the temple-city is cannot plan a siege of it, and because the Daughters of Khaine can move through mist that stops everybody else.

Scouts who learn the Veil’s habits become the most valuable soldiers in any army operating here, and command them accordingly. Scouts who do not learn simply stop reporting, and the war carries on without the information they were sent to bring back.`,

  zoli: `A dark island sitting alone at the eastern edge of the Umbral Veil, large enough to be named and small enough that the name is all anyone has to say about it. It is a step. Anything crossing from Hellezar into the Veil that does not want to make the passage in one leg puts in at Zoli, which is why a garrison here is worth more than the island is.`,

  mirrorshade: `The Misted Isles, a cluster of low islands inside the Umbral Veil where the shadow sits so thick on the water that the isles reflect each other and little else. Crews report sailing the same channel twice and arriving somewhere different.

They are the last shelter before Hagg Nar’s waters. An attacking fleet has to stage through the Misted Isles or attempt the Veil in one crossing, and the Daughters of Khaine know which option a tired admiral takes.`,

  harrowdeep: `A warren of drowned passages under the Umbral Veil, where the realm’s shadow has worn through into something older and the geography stops being reliable at all. The Harrowdeep is entered, not travelled: parties go in at one place and come out somewhere they did not choose.

Everyone who can afford to avoid it does. Everyone who cannot — raiders, fugitives, and anybody who needs to be on the other side of the Veil before the week is out — takes the risk, and the survivors come back with things worth the losses often enough to keep the traffic going.`,

  'ashen-veil': `The Helleflux Ashspires, a stand of black spires above Hagg Nar where the ground vents something hot and sulphurous into air that is otherwise cold. The Daughters of Khaine work iron here, and the smoke that rises from the spires is the one thing in the Umbral Veil that can be seen from outside it.

The Ashspires are why the temple-city is where it is. Take them and Hagg Nar makes its own weapons on somebody else’s schedule.`,

  'hagg-nar': `Morathi’s first and greatest temple-city, raised at the centre of the Umbral Veil, where she taught the surviving witch-cults of Khaine how to move through the darkest currents in the realm. Beneath its streets, in the depths of Khruthú, stands the Mother Cauldron, Máthcoir, where the souls she won were reshaped into the first Melusai and Khinerai.

The city outgrew its own temper almost at once. Hag Queens turned their rivalries into street-fighting, and Morathi answered by sending war covens out to hunt shards of Khaine and found temples of their own rather than let them settle the question at home. Every sect the Daughters of Khaine have raised since — across every realm — still names Hagg Nar the capital of the creed.

That loyalty makes the city a weapon rather than a shrine. Every aelf sworn to Khaine is expected to walk these streets at least once, which means whoever holds Hagg Nar holds the one place a fractured order still agrees on. It is also the best-defended ground in Ulgu, because the Veil does most of the defending.`,

  'aile-ti': `An enclosed island south-east of Hagg Nar, ringed by its own weather and named in the old aelven manner, which on this plate usually marks something that predates the current owners. It is close enough to the temple-city to matter and far enough to be deniable. Forces that want to be near Hagg Nar without being at Hagg Nar put in at Aile-Ti.`,

  vochtai: `Vochtai, the Isle of Veiled Threats — a substantial island on the Umbral Veil’s eastern rim, and one of the few places the cartographer bothered to editorialise about. Whatever the threats are, they are considered plural and considered veiled.

It commands the channel between the Veil and Hellezar. An army holding Vochtia can close that channel, and every army that has tried to hold it has found out what the name is warning about.`,

  // --- Hellezar ------------------------------------------------------------
  'nobles-height': `A spired city on the high ground at Hellezar’s northern edge, seat of whichever of the dominion’s aelven houses is currently winning. The Height changes hands by inheritance, marriage and poison rather than by siege, and the walls have hardly been tested in living memory.

That untested quality is the point. An army that actually assaults Noble’s Height would be the first in centuries, and the houses inside have spent those centuries assuming nobody would.`,

  hellezar: `The dominion of the realm’s aelven nobility, a dark upland country of estates, feuds and very long memories. Hellezar produces more of Ulgu’s sorcerers and more of its assassins than anywhere else, and does not consider these separate industries.

Strategically it is the bridge between the Umbral Veil and Greater Ulguroth — the ground an army crosses when it wants to move between Morathi’s power and Malerion’s. Its houses have grown rich charging for that passage and would fight to keep charging for it.`,

  alidi: `A small settlement on Hellezar’s northern moors, unremarkable except for standing on the one road east that does not go through Noble’s Height. Everything that wants to avoid the great houses comes through Alidi. The great houses know this and let it continue, which tells you what the traffic is worth to them.`,

  'lake-depthless': `A lake in north-eastern Hellezar that has never been sounded, fed by the Contrarian River and draining nowhere anyone has found. The water is clear, cold and reportedly without a bottom. It is the only fresh water of any size in the north-east. An army campaigning here drinks from the Depthless or turns around.`,

  'contrarian-river': `A river that runs the wrong way. The Contrarian drains uphill out of the eastern marches and into Lake Depthless, and has done so for as long as anyone has charted it; the realm has never offered an explanation. Barge traffic uses it precisely because of that, carrying cargo inland without haulage. Whoever holds the river holds the cheapest supply line in eastern Ulgu.`,

  'tarpit-lakes': `A string of pale lakes in the far north-east that are not water. Things that go into the Tarpits stay there, preserved, and the locals dig them out again when the price of preserved things is high enough. Armies avoid the ground and covet the edges of it. The tar is a siege material, and there is nowhere else in the realm to get it in quantity.`,

  'fort-mirage': `A fortress in central Hellezar that is reliably reported in three different places. Whether the Mirage moves, or the approaches do, is the sort of question Hellezar’s sorcerers refuse to settle for anyone who has not paid. Practically, it means the fort cannot be invested. Every attempt to besiege Fort Mirage has ended with the besiegers camped around ground the fort was not on.`,

  'tai-kos': `A broken tangle of channels and low islands on Hellezar’s western shore, the natural landing ground for anything crossing out of the Umbral Veil. It is the dominion’s soft edge. Hellezar’s houses keep Tai-Kos garrisoned out of a shared understanding that whatever comes out of the Veil will come out here.`,

  'scaleblight-coast': `Hellezar’s southern shoreline, named for the rot that gets into anything scaled that swims near it. The fishing died generations ago and the villages that lived on it turned to wrecking instead. For an army it is a coast with no forage and no welcome, which is why campaigns in Hellezar march inland and stay there.`,

  smittencove: `A port on the Scaleblight Coast, the only one that still works. Smittencove survives on the traffic between Hellezar and the Convergence and on a harbour deep enough for the ships that carry it. Take Smittencove and Hellezar’s nobility has to move everything overland, through their own feuding neighbours. That is usually the point of taking it.`,

  'the-tithe': `A dark island in the strait west of Hellezar, named for what passes across it rather than for anything on it. Cargo moving between the dominions is landed here, counted, and taxed by whoever currently has soldiers standing on the beach. The arrangement has survived every change of ownership because everyone who takes the island keeps the practice. It is the cheapest revenue in the realm.`,

  'nemesa': `A town south of Hagg Nar, on the road between the Umbral Veil and Hellezar’s heartland. It is a staging post with a temple’s name and a garrison’s habits. Anything the Daughters of Khaine send east passes through Nemesa, and anything Hellezar sends west stops here first to find out what it is walking into.`,

  'battle-of-druchxar': `A battlefield marked on the plate and left unexplained, in the open country between Hagg Nar and the Erekhui Mountains. Something was decided here at a scale worth recording. The ground still matters for the same reason it mattered then: it is the widest usable crossing between the Veil and the eastern dominions, and armies keep arriving at it because there is nowhere else to arrive.`,

  'erekhui-mountains': `A mountain range walling off Hellezar from the Shadrac Convergence proper, steep, cold and short of passes. The Erekhui are the reason Hellezar’s politics stayed Hellezar’s problem for as long as they did. There are two ways through that a supply train can use. Both of them have been fought over more than once.`,

  'ulthern-citadel': `A castle on the Erekhui’s western flank, watching the passes. Ulthern is old, undermanned and sited well enough that this has never mattered. Whoever holds the Citadel decides which army gets to cross the mountains this season. It has changed hands rarely and always expensively.`,

  // --- Greater Ulguroth ----------------------------------------------------
  tanglebreach: `A pass in the northern heights where the shadow snarls into knots and then, once a season, tears open. The breach is marked on the plate as a battlefield because armies keep timing their campaigns to it. Hold Tanglebreach when it opens and an army is through the northern wall in a day. Miss the window and the same march takes a month.`,

  'quintaron-spear': `A stone spire driven through the northern marches of Greater Ulguroth, of a make that does not match anything Malerion’s people build. Nobody has moved it. It is the reference point every road in the north-east is measured from, which makes it the first thing a surveying army looks for and the first thing a defending one would like to hide.`,

  sufferance: `A town in the north-east of Malerion’s dominion, named for the terms on which it exists. Sufferance pays its tithes, asks nothing, and is left alone. That arrangement makes it the natural meeting ground for anybody in the east who needs to talk without Druchiroth listening — or at least without Druchiroth admitting it is listening.`,

  'silver-tongue': `A river in the far north-east, bright against dark ground and audible a long way off. The Silver Tongue is the only navigable water inland in this quarter of the realm. Every force operating in the north-east is fed up the Tongue or is not fed at all.`,

  'koschaic-demise': `A site in the eastern uplands where something considerable ended. The plate names it and offers nothing further, in the manner of a cartographer recording a thing everyone local already knows. Armies camp here because the ground is clear and nothing grows on it. They tend not to camp here twice.`,

  malevolus: `A town on Greater Ulguroth’s far eastern shore, at the edge of the charted realm. Beyond it the plate simply stops. Malevolus is the last place an eastward campaign can resupply, which makes it either the most important town in the dominion or the least, depending entirely on whether anyone is going east this year.`,

  slivern: `A narrow dark island off the eastern coast, long and thin and sitting directly across the sea lane. It is a watchpost. A garrison on Slivern sees everything moving along the eastern shore three days before the shore does.`,

  'coast-of-antipathy': `The eastern seaboard of Malerion’s dominion, where the land and the water have agreed not to cooperate. There is no shelf, no shelter and no beach worth the name for its whole length. It is the reason Greater Ulguroth has never been invaded from the east. The coast does the work a wall would have to do.`,

  ulguroth: `The largest of Ulgu’s Thirteen Dominions, sprawling across the eastern Shadrac Convergence as Malerion’s own personal domain. Its capital, Druchiroth, is where the Lord of Shadow holds court, and nobody enters Ulguroth uninvited without his shadow-warriors already knowing they are there.

Druchiroth predates Malerion’s rule as a god among a pantheon. He and Morathi raised the citadel together with their own sorcery in the Age of Myth, before either had met the deities they would later found the Pantheon of Order alongside, and the city has stood through every alliance and falling-out since.

The other twelve Dominions answer to their own dark sorcerers, dream-seers and robber-princes, and all twelve ultimately pay homage back to the court that built the first of them. An army that wants to change who rules Ulgu has to come here eventually, and everything between the Umbral Veil and the eastern coast is arranged to make that journey as long as possible.`,

  'fort-intrinsic': `A fortress on the northern road into Malerion’s dominion, built where the ground allows exactly one approach. Intrinsic is not large; it does not need to be. It is the first thing any northern invasion has to reduce, and reducing it takes long enough for Druchiroth to hear about it.`,

  'suspected-eshin-lair': `A marked site in Greater Ulguroth’s interior where the Clans Eshin are believed to keep a warren. The plate hedges, which is itself informative: somebody surveyed this ground, found what they found, and declined to put a firmer word on the map.

Skaven in Malerion’s own dominion are a standing embarrassment and a standing threat. The Lord of Shadow’s forces sweep the area periodically and the lair is still suspected rather than former.`,

  'the-ulgish-cairns': `A field of raised stones in the eastern interior, older than Malerion’s rule and unexplained by it. The cairns are left alone by local custom and by the sort of thing that happens to people who do not. They are also the only cover on an otherwise open plain, which means armies keep having to decide how superstitious they are.`,

  'malerions-veins': `A branching river system running down through Greater Ulguroth, named for the Lord of Shadow and treated as an extension of him. Traffic on the Veins is watched; everyone assumes it is watched by Malerion personally, and behaves accordingly. They are the dominion’s arteries in the plain sense too. An army that cuts the Veins cuts Druchiroth’s supply from its southern provinces.`,

  'glib-spire': `A tall spike of stone beside the Veins, named for the talking it is said to do. Travellers report advice; the advice is reported to be good and expensive. It is a waypoint on the southern river road, and whatever else it is, it is the last dry landmark before the ground turns.`,

  'arch-of-dark-miracles': `A freestanding arch on the eastern flank of Malerion’s dominion, of no known construction, through which things sometimes happen that did not happen on the other side. Pilgrims come. Not all of them leave in the same condition. It is a realmgate in everything but name, and the reason a fortress-dominion keeps a garrison on an otherwise worthless stretch of moor.`,

  'emanatus-gnawsite': `A marked site in the south-east where something has been eating the realm. The damage spreads slowly, in a pattern, and the plate flags it with a warding rune rather than a settlement mark. Malerion’s people contain it rather than cure it. An enemy that breaks the containment does more harm to Greater Ulguroth in a season than an army would in a year.`,

  // --- Zoktai and the western reaches --------------------------------------
  chuloraine: `A long dark headland on the realm’s western edge, the last land before the plate runs out. Chuloraine is thinly held and heavily watched. It is where things arrive from outside Ulgu that would rather not arrive at a port. Every smuggling route in the west starts on this coast.`,

  'mehemai-clash': `A battlefield in northern Zoktai, marked with crossed blades and nothing else. Two forces met here in enough strength to put it on the chart. The ground is a crossroads — the only one in the northern dominion where the mist stays thin enough to manoeuvre in formation. That is why it was fought over and why it will be again.`,

  zoktai: `A broad dark dominion filling the realm’s west, all moor, mesa and shadow-forest, governed loosely from a dozen small holds that do not much like each other. Zoktai is poor, large and awkward to cross. Its value is position. Everything moving between the Penumbral Sea and the Shadrac Sea goes through Zoktai or goes the long way round, and the long way round takes a season.`,

  'stonescry-mesas': `Flat-topped stone tables standing above the Zoktai moor, the highest ground in the west. Sound behaves oddly on them, which is where the name comes from and why signal stations were built up there. An army holding the Mesas can see and be heard across half the dominion. In Ulgu that is closer to a strategic weapon than a convenience.`,

  'slaanguan-peaks': `A ridge in central Zoktai where a Slaaneshi incursion left its mark on the rock and did not entirely leave. The peaks are shunned by the locals and garrisoned by nobody. They are also the only pass through the dominion’s spine that a supply train can use. Every campaign in the west eventually has to decide whether to risk them.`,

  anchorpoint: `A harbour town on the Zoktai coast, named with the west’s usual bluntness. It is the one reliable landing on the Ontu Stretch’s northern shore. Everything invading or reinforcing Zoktai from the sea lands at Anchorpoint, and the town has been sacked often enough to have stopped building anything it minds losing.`,

  yndaxil: `A settlement on an island off Zoktai’s western coast, small, old and entirely self-sufficient by choice. It survives by being of no use to anybody. In the west that is a recognised strategy and Yndaxil is the standing example of it working.`,

  'zortil-kharr': `A stronghold in the Slaanguan foothills, cut into the rock rather than built on it. Zortil Kharr watches the southern approach to the peaks and has done for longer than the current holders have existed. It cannot be starved — the works go deep — and it cannot be stormed except through one gate. Campaigns in Zoktai are planned around not having to take it.`,

  'crumbling-spine': `A ridge running east from Zoktai into the Convergence, weathering apart faster than it should. Sections of the Spine collapse without notice and the road across it is rebuilt every few seasons. It is still the fastest way east. Armies use it and accept the losses, and the losses are usually to the ridge rather than to the enemy.`,

  'forest-of-lost-sailors': `A shadow-forest inland of the Zoktai coast where the trees have grown up through the wrecks that the mist carried inland during whatever happened here. Hulls are still visible in the canopy. It is impassable to formed troops and excellent for everyone else. Raiders operating against the coast road base themselves in it because nothing heavy can follow them in.`,

  'aiza-gate': `A realmgate on Zoktai’s eastern march, marked with the gate sigil the plate uses for the real ones. Where it goes is not written down here. A working gate in Ulgu is worth a dominion. Whatever holds Aiza Gate can reinforce the west from somewhere else entirely, which is the only reason anyone has ever won a war in Zoktai quickly.`,

  'gallach-sparn': `A range of broken uplands between Zoktai and the central Convergence, thinly grassed and thinly populated. The Sparn is herding country when it is anything. Armies cross it because it is the direct route and regret it because there is nothing to eat. Every campaign through Gallach Sparn is a campaign about its own baggage train.`,

  'smugglers-joy': `A port on the Ontu Stretch, honest about its trade in the way that only somewhere with no other trade can afford to be. The Joy handles the cargo the Convergence’s respectable harbours decline. It is well defended, because its clientele insists. An army that wants the western sea lanes has to buy it, take it, or work around it, and the first option is usually cheapest.`,

  oilfort: `A works on the Ontu Stretch’s northern shore that renders shadewhale and worse into lamp oil, engine oil and the black stuff that Ulgu’s sorcerers will not name. The stink reaches Smuggler’s Joy on a bad day. It is the only industrial plant of any size in the west. Whoever holds Oilfort keeps the western fleets lit and moving; whoever burns it has ended the campaigning season for everybody.`,

  'proboscis-iii': `A long tapering peninsula reaching into the Ontu Stretch, the third such finger on this coast and numbered accordingly by a surveyor who had run out of patience. It is a natural mole. A fleet sheltering behind Proboscis III is out of the weather and out of sight, which is worth more here than a harbour.`,

  // --- Caizan and the Ontu Stretch -----------------------------------------
  caizan: `A dominion at the realm’s south-western edge, half of it off the plate entirely. What is charted is dark, coastal and quiet. Caizan’s courts keep out of the Convergence’s arguments and have done so successfully for a long time. Their neutrality is the reason everyone fighting in the west still has somewhere to sell plunder.`,

  'ontu-stretch': `A long pale sea running east to west across the realm’s middle, separating Zoktai from Klarondu and the southern dominions. The Stretch is shallow, crowded with islands and busier than any water in Ulgu except the Penumbral.

It is the realm’s main road. Everything that moves in quantity between west and east moves on the Ontu Stretch, and the wars fought over it have always really been wars over the traffic.`,

  'leathanam-wastes': `A stretch of dead coast on the Stretch’s southern shore, scoured to bare ground by whatever the mist does here. Nothing is farmed and nothing is built. It is the one place on the southern shore where an army can land in strength unopposed, because there is nothing there to oppose it and nothing there afterwards to feed it.`,

  'dolorous-fens': `The Mistveil Traps — a belt of sink and standing water on the Ontu Stretch’s southern edge, where the mist lies low enough to hide the ground under it. Locals move through on marked paths that are not marked for outsiders. Armies have drowned in the Traps in numbers. Every attack on Barak-Mhornar from the west has had to either buy a guide or go around, and going around adds a fortnight.`,

  'barak-mhornar': `A duardin sea-hold on the Ontu Stretch’s southern shore, the Kharadron trading post that keeps the realm’s western sky lanes open. Barak-Mhornar is built the way duardin build: vertical, sealed, and with a great deal more under it than above.

It is also the only place in western Ulgu where a contract is enforced by something other than reputation. The sky-fleets moor here, the Traps protect the landward approach, and the hold charges everybody for the privilege of both.

Taking it would hand an army the western Stretch, its shipping and its sky traffic in one stroke. The duardin are entirely aware of this, which is why the hold looks the way it does.`,

  slitherdwell: `A settlement on the southern shore, built low and long over water that moves. The name is a description rather than a warning, but it functions as both. It sits on the coast road between Barak-Mhornar and the Gate of Vipers, which is the only stretch of that road where a column can be ambushed from below.`,

  'gate-of-vipers': `A realmgate on the Ontu Stretch’s south-eastern shore, marked with the gate sigil and named for what came through it or what guards it — the plate does not say which. It is the southern shore’s fast route to somewhere else. Control of the Gate has decided every campaign fought along this coast, usually before the campaign started.`,

  'ostensible-mountains': `A range on Klarondu’s northern border that is not reliably there. Surveyors have charted the Ostensible Mountains four times and produced four ranges; armies have marched at them and arrived somewhere flat. They are nonetheless a border. Whatever the mountains are doing, they do it consistently enough that Klarondu’s northern frontier has never needed a garrison.`,

  'manna-fields': `Ground on the northern edge of Klarondu that produces food in a realm that mostly does not. What grows on the Manna Fields grows in the dark and is edible, which is enough. They are the reason Klarondu can field an army at all. Every plan for taking the southern dominion begins with the Fields, and every plan for holding it begins with denying them.`,

  'scant-hope': `A settlement between the Manna Fields and the coast, named by somebody who had looked at the surrounding country. It is a waystation with a well. It is on the only road from the Fields to the sea. Whoever holds Scant Hope decides where Klarondu’s harvest goes.`,

  // --- Va-Leth and the central Convergence ---------------------------------
  'va-leth': `A central dominion of dark upland and cut coastline, lying between the Penumbral Sea and the Inner Shadrac. Va-Leth is the crossroads of the realm’s northern half: four dominions border it and none of them can reach the others without it.

Its lords have traded on that for centuries, selling passage and selling information about who has bought passage. An army that takes Va-Leth does not gain much ground. It gains everybody else’s road.`,

  'mountains-of-maul': `A range across northern Va-Leth, high, cold and named for what happens in the passes. The Maul is the dominion’s northern wall and the reason the Penumbral Sea’s traffic has to land where Va-Leth says. There are three passes. All three are watched, and the watching is the dominion’s principal industry.`,

  ironshard: `Ironshard, called the Rothtor — a stronghold in the Mountains of Maul, built of and into the black iron-bearing rock the range is named for. It works the ore, arms the dominion, and answers to Va-Leth’s lords when it feels like it.

It is the only significant arms manufactory between Hellezar and the western sea. Campaigns in the centre of the realm are supplied out of Ironshard or are supplied slowly.`,

  glimpsewoad: `A shadow-forest on Va-Leth’s northern slopes, where the trees thin at intervals and something can be seen through them. What is seen is not agreed upon. Practically it is cover on a mountain flank, and both sides of any fight in the Maul try to put light troops into it.`,

  'traders-massacre': `A site on Va-Leth’s northern road where a caravan was destroyed comprehensively enough to be put on the map. The plate marks it and moves on. The road still runs through it, because there is no other road. Escorts are hired at higher rates for this stretch and the rates have never come down.`,

  'greycrawl-inlets': `A run of narrow flooded valleys on Va-Leth’s north-western shore, where the sea works inland further each year. The Inlets are tidal, mazy and navigable by small craft only. They are the back door into Va-Leth. Anything that wants to land in the dominion without going through the Mountains of Maul comes up the Greycrawl.`,

  'arcanite-veldt': `Open ground in southern Va-Leth where the magic lies close to the surface and the grass grows in patterns. Sorcerers quarry the Veldt for reagents; armies cross it quickly. It is the only place in the dominion where cavalry can be used properly. Every battle fought in southern Va-Leth has been fought here for that reason.`,

  'the-slash': `A deep cut across the southern Veldt, straight enough to be artificial and old enough that nobody claims to have made it. It runs for miles and is crossable in two places. Those two crossings are the whole tactical story of southern Va-Leth.`,

  'dhucha-zhur': `A spired city on the southern edge of Va-Leth, looking out over the Coast of Duplicity. Dhucha Zhur is the dominion’s largest settlement and the seat of whoever is currently collecting the passage tolls.

It is wealthy, well built and badly sited — an enemy that gets past the Mountains of Maul is already most of the way to its gates. The city compensates with walls and with the general understanding that sacking it would cost more in trade than it would yield in plunder.`,

  'coast-of-duplicity': `Va-Leth’s southern shore, facing the Inner Shadrac. The coast is charted, and the charts do not hold: bays close, headlands move, and pilots who have worked it for twenty years still take soundings. Every fleet crossing to the realm’s centre has to come ashore somewhere along it. That is the entire reason the dominion above it is worth anything.`,

  inzitil: `A settlement on the Coast of Duplicity, at one of the few landings the coast keeps in the same place. Inzitil exists to receive what the coast permits to land. It is small, fortified, and the first objective of any assault on Va-Leth from the south.`,

  // --- The Inner Shadrac ---------------------------------------------------
  ulgu: `The Realm of Shadow, seen from its own centre. This is the Shadrac Convergence — the point where the realm’s great mist-currents wind into one another, drawn on the plate as a ring that the cartography acknowledges and does not explain.

Ulgu is not a place that holds still to be mapped. The dominions around this ring shift their distances from one another, the seas between them widen and close, and a campaign that looks like a week’s march on paper is a month in the field or a day, without warning and without pattern. What the Convergence gives an army is the one fixed thing in a realm with nothing else fixed: from here, everywhere else is at least reachable.

Malerion rules the east of it and Morathi the west, and the arrangement has held for as long as it has because neither can march on the other without crossing this ground first. Every war for Ulgu is a war for the Convergence, whatever the combatants say they are fighting over.`,

  'uhl-gysh': `The Inner Shadrac, the water and mist immediately around the Convergence ring — the realm’s hub, and the one stretch of Ulgu where all thirteen dominions are, in theory, equidistant.

In practice the Inner Shadrac is where the realm’s distortion is strongest and strangest, and the fleets that work it navigate by agreement rather than by instrument. Misthávn moors at its western edge for exactly that reason: a city that can move is worth more here than a city that cannot.

Nobody holds it. Holding it would mean holding mist. What armies fight for instead are the rims — Cape Tenebrax, Spindlespike, the Coast of Duplicity — because whoever holds three points on the rim controls who crosses the middle.`,

  'cape-tenebrax': `A headland on the Inner Shadrac’s western rim, the anchorage Misthávn moors off. The Cape is the closest thing the floating city has to a fixed address. It is also the only solid ground within a long sail of the realm’s centre, which makes it the natural objective for anyone who wants the Inner Shadrac and has no ships to spare.`,

  misthavn: `Misthávn, the City of Scoundrels — a floating settlement of moored ships riding off Cape Tenebrax, its districts able to scatter across the water and reassemble somewhere else as circumstances require. It is the largest population centre in the realm and it has no walls, because it has no need of a fixed position to defend.

A Grand Conclave nominally rules it. The council is as morally flexible as the rogues, Grey Wizards and fleet-commanders who fill its ranks, and Azyrite officials have never once made their writ stick on its decks. Every armada in the city answers to its own commander — Scourge Privateer, Freeguild captain, duardin shipwright — long before it answers to the Conclave.

Every illicit trade the realms produce passes through Misthávn at some point, from stolen Chaos weapons to the narcotics that spread from its own black markets to half the Free Cities beyond Ulgu’s border. Its people intend to keep it exactly that lawless. Taking the city is not a matter of breaching anything; it is a matter of catching it, and so far nobody with the strength to try has found it worth the effort.`,

  spindlespike: `A rock tower standing out of the Inner Shadrac on its eastern rim, visible from the whole basin when the mist allows and the only fixed navigational mark in the realm’s centre. Every crossing of the Inner Shadrac is plotted off Spindlespike. A garrison on it can lie about what it signals, and has.`,

  'curlicue-sea': `A vast slow spiral of water and shadow south of the Inner Shadrac, turning on itself for a hundred miles. Ships entering the Curlicue at the wrong point are carried inward and take weeks to work back out. Knowing the spiral is a trade. The pilots who sell that knowledge are based in Misthávn, and the price of crossing the realm’s centre is, in practice, whatever they say it is.`,

  // --- The Shadrac Sea and the southern coast ------------------------------
  'cape-heartpierce': `A long spur of land on the western side of the Curlicue, pointing into it. The Cape is exposed, treeless and constantly in the wind that the spiral generates. It is the western limit of the Shadrac Sea’s shipping and the last shelter before the Curlicue. Every southbound fleet stops here to decide whether it is going on.`,

  'deathbite-point': `A headland south of Cape Heartpierce with a rock shelf under it that has taken more hulls than the Cruel Splinters. The Point is marked on every chart and still claims ships. Whoever holds it runs the only light on the southern approach, and lights can be moved.`,

  'itril-gharr': `A settlement at the root of Deathbite Point, living on what the Point produces. Wrecking is not admitted to and is not seriously denied. It is the only harbour on this coast. Southern campaigns supply through Itril Gharr because the alternative is not supplying at all.`,

  'the-lost-shine': `A place on the southern coast where something luminous used to be and is not any longer. The plate records the absence, which is a strange thing for a chart to do. Pilgrims and prospectors still come. The ground is picked over and the traffic keeps the coast road open, which is the only reason an army would care.`,

  'khieli-gadh': `The site of the Grand Khieli-Gadh, a gathering of the realm’s aelven hosts significant enough that the plate records the field rather than the settlement. What was agreed or decided here is not written on the map.

The ground is a natural amphitheatre in otherwise broken country — the one place on the southern shore where thirty thousand people could stand and see each other. It is used for the same purpose still, which makes it the place an army goes to be seen arriving.`,

  obfuscatia: `A belt of country on the southern coast where the shadow is thickest at ground level, named with the realm’s usual directness. Maps of Obfuscatia are sold with a disclaimer. It is the gap in the southern coast’s defences that nobody has ever successfully exploited, because nobody has ever successfully got through it in formation.`,

  'holloweye-caves': `A cave system in the southern cliffs, extensive, inhabited, and not by anything that identifies itself. The eyes the name refers to are reported by everybody who goes in and described differently by each of them. The caves run under the coast road for miles. An army moving along that road is moving over somebody else’s ground and knows it.`,

  'shadrac-coast': `The northern shore of the Shadrac Sea, running along Klarondu’s edge. It is the realm’s busiest coastline after the Penumbral, and the only one with a continuous road. That road is Klarondu’s lifeline. Every campaign in the south-west has been a campaign to cut it.`,

  'shadrac-sea': `The great southern water that gives the Convergence its name, lying between Klarondu, Graich Hai and the realm’s centre. It is broad, dark and comparatively honest: the Shadrac behaves like a sea rather than like Ulgu.

It is therefore where the realm’s fleets actually fight. Battles that would be impossible in the Umbral Veil or the Curlicue are possible here, and both Malerion’s and Morathi’s admirals have used it accordingly.`,

  'maddening-swirl': `A smaller spiral off the Shadrac Sea’s eastern shore, of the same kind as the Curlicue and worse tempered. Crews caught in the Maddening Swirl come out changed if they come out. It is avoidable. Fleets that avoid it add three days to the eastern crossing, which is precisely the delay a defender is counting on.`,

  // --- Graich Hai ----------------------------------------------------------
  'graich-hai': `A southern dominion of mist-forest and cliff, running down the realm’s southern spine. Graich Hai is the least travelled of the Thirteen and keeps it that way. Its value is that it touches everything — the Shadrac Sea, the Ulguroth Spiral, the southern coast — without being easy to cross from any of them. An army that establishes itself in Graich Hai is awkward to remove and awkward to ignore.`,

  'mistspite-cliffs': `A wall of cliff along Graich Hai’s western edge, where the mist comes up from below rather than settling from above. The updraught is strong enough to be dangerous to anything flying. It closes the dominion to sky-fleets, which is why the Kharadron of Barak-Mhornar have never extended their routes south.`,

  xoil: `A settlement on an island in the southern shallows, dark, small and old. Xoil is on no route to anywhere. It is a place to put something one does not want found. Both sides in most of the realm’s wars have used it for that, usually without telling each other.`,

  'aich-nex': `A settlement in southern Graich Hai, at the foot of the Untold Peaks. It is the last inhabited place before the mountains. Anything crossing the southern range stages out of Aich Nex, and the village’s entire economy is guides, mules and the knowledge of which passes are currently real.`,

  'untold-peaks': `A mountain range in the far south that has never been fully surveyed, because the surveys disagree. The Untold Peaks are named for the count nobody can settle. They close the realm’s southern edge. Whatever is on the other side has not come through in recorded memory, and Graich Hai’s people would prefer to keep the arrangement.`,

  // --- Klarondu ------------------------------------------------------------
  'hellebrach-khai': `A spired city in northern Klarondu, and the dominion’s principal fortress. Hellebrach Khai is built on the one hill in a country of plains, and everything about Klarondu’s defence assumes that fact. It has never fallen to assault. It has changed hands twice by negotiation, which its lords regard as an entirely different matter.`,

  'myrantoc-embassy': `A compound on Klarondu’s northern plain maintained by an outside power that the plate names and does not explain. The Embassy is walled, garrisoned and pointedly not part of the dominion around it. Its existence is the reason Klarondu’s politics have an outside dimension at all. Every faction in the dominion has been in through those gates and none of them talk about it.`,

  'the-kraithes-revenge': `A battlefield on the northern plain, marked with crossed blades and named for a grievance that the map does not detail. Something was settled here, at length. The ground is the junction of the two roads that cross Klarondu. It was fought over for that reason and the reason has not changed.`,

  'dissipating-dunes': `A belt of shifting ground on Klarondu’s western edge, where the shadow thins and the land underneath turns out to be sand. The dunes move, and the maps of them are redrawn each season. They are impassable to wheeled transport. Klarondu’s western flank has never needed a wall because the Dunes are one.`,

  'caengan-lodge': `A hunting lodge on the western plain, fortified the way a lodge is fortified when its owners expect to use it as a strongpoint. It belongs to Klarondu’s nobility and is where they gather when the dominion has a decision to make. An army that took the Lodge would not take much ground and would take the dominion’s command structure with it.`,

  'mask-of-eliathe': `A carved face in the stone of central Klarondu, enormous and old, of a style that predates every current claim to the dominion. The plate marks it as a site rather than a ruin. It is the landmark that Klarondu’s roads are measured from, and the muster point everybody in the dominion already knows how to find.`,

  klarondu: `A large southern dominion of open plain, shadow-grass and small feuding holds, running from the Ontu Stretch down to the Shadrac Sea. Klarondu is the most agriculturally productive ground in Ulgu, which in this realm is a low bar and still decisive.

It has no natural defences except the Dissipating Dunes on one flank and distance on the other. What it has instead is the Manna Fields, Hellebrach Khai, and a nobility that has spent generations learning to change sides at the right moment.

Every long campaign in Ulgu eventually needs Klarondu, because armies have to eat. That, and not any ambition of its own, is why the dominion has been invaded more often than any other.`,

  'mandraich-plain': `The open western plain of Klarondu, flat, featureless and the only ground in the realm where a battle can be fought the way the drill books describe. Both sides in any war for the dominion try to force the decision here, and both sides know the other is trying, which is why it so rarely happens.`,

  behest: `A settlement in central Klarondu, garrisoned and named for the terms it holds its charter on. Behest does what it is told by whoever currently holds Hellebrach Khai. It sits on the crossroads of the plain. Its usefulness is entirely that it will do the same thing for the next occupier.`,

  'vale-of-broken-hopes': `A valley on Klarondu’s southern edge, running down towards the Shadrac Sea. An army came up it once and did not come out the other end; the name is the memorial. It is still the direct route from the coast to the plain. Armies still use it, and the ones that do post a great deal more flank security than the first one did.`,

  'cellach-visage': `A second carved face, north of the Mask of Eliathe and of the same make, watching the other way. The pair of them bracket Klarondu’s heartland. The Visage overlooks the road from the Ontu Stretch. Whatever it was built for, it is now the observation post for the dominion’s northern approach.`,

  'split-crust': `Ground in southern Klarondu where the surface has cracked open in long parallel seams. What is underneath is warm and has not been surveyed. The seams break up the terrain badly enough that no formed body of troops crosses it. Every road in the south goes around the Split Crust, which makes the roads predictable.`,

  'the-great-suffocation': `A region on Klarondu’s south-western corner where the shadow lies so heavy that the air under it will not support a fire. Lamps go out; so, in time, do people. It is a hole in the map that armies have to march around. The detour is four days and has decided at least one campaign on its own.`,

  'fallen-virtue': `A ruin at the realm’s south-western corner, of something large that was abandoned rather than destroyed. The name is the only comment the plate offers. It is the last standing structure before the Great Suffocation, which makes it the natural forward base for anyone who intends to go anywhere near it.`,

  desperance: `A settlement on the southern edge of Klarondu, beyond the crossroads and short of the coast. Desperance is a garrison town that outlived its garrison. It holds the road that skirts the Great Suffocation. That is the whole of its strategic existence and the entire reason it still has walls.`,

  'ulgus-tendril': `A finger of pure shadow reaching down out of the realm’s interior into the southern plain, dense enough to be mapped as terrain. The Tendril moves, slowly, and the maps are updated when it does. It is the realm itself behaving like a feature. Armies treat it as impassable and plan around where it is expected to be next season, which is a peculiarly Ulguan kind of staff work.`,

  // --- The Ulguroth Spiral -------------------------------------------------
  'yntoch-promontory': `A headland at the northern end of the Ulguroth Spiral, jutting into the Emynne Sea. It is the first solid ground a fleet coming south out of Hellezar can use. Whoever holds the Promontory sees the whole northern approach to the Spiral and can put troops ashore behind anybody using it.`,

  'the-notches': `A settlement in a series of cuts in the Promontory’s ridge, each one a natural fortification. The Notches is less a town than a string of positions that agreed to share a name. It is the strongpoint the Promontory is held with. Taking it means taking each notch separately, which is exactly why it was built this way.`,

  'emynne-sea': `A narrow sea between Hellezar’s southern coast and the Ulguroth Spiral, sheltered on both sides and comparatively calm. The Emynne is the eastern realm’s inland waterway. It is how Malerion moves troops between his northern and southern provinces without crossing the Spiral. Cutting it is the first objective of any campaign against Greater Ulguroth from the west.`,

  'ravage-of-luxcious': `A battlefield on the Spiral’s north-western rim, marked with crossed blades. Luxcious was a place before it was an event; the plate now records only the event. The ground is the western entrance to the Spiral. Everything that has ever tried to enter the Spiral from the Inner Shadrac has had to come through here first.`,

  'ruins-of-neff-taal': `A ruined city on the Spiral’s western edge, substantial enough that the plate marks the whole footprint. Neff-Taal was something considerable and is now stone. The ruins are the only cover on a bare rim and the only source of building material within a week’s haul. Every army that has campaigned in the western Spiral has quarried them.`,

  'khak-quenta-reaches': `Broken upland on the Spiral’s western rim, cut by dry channels running in towards the centre. The Reaches are the natural line of advance into the Spiral and are held accordingly. The channels are deep enough to hide a column and narrow enough to trap one. Fighting here is a matter of who gets into which channel first.`,

  'harsh-welcome': `A settlement at the head of the Khak-Quenta Reaches, named by its founders and confirmed by everybody who has arrived since. It is a fortified customs post with a town attached. Everything entering the Spiral from the west is counted here. Everything that objects to being counted is dealt with here too.`,

  'choking-neck': `A narrows on the Spiral’s western rim where the passable ground pinches to a few hundred yards between cliff and sink. There is no way round that does not add a week. It is the single most defensible position in the eastern half of the realm, and Malerion’s people have never bothered to fortify it, on the grounds that the ground does the work.`,

  'windshaden-plains': `Open ground on the Spiral’s northern rim, swept constantly by the wind the Spiral generates. Nothing grows above knee height and nothing stays where it is put. It is the only ground in the Spiral where an army can move quickly. It is also the only ground where it can be seen doing so.`,

  'coldhearth-peaks': `A cold range on the Spiral’s inner rim, snow-covered in a realm that mostly is not. The Coldhearth are the highest ground in eastern Ulgu. They divide the Spiral’s northern arm from its centre. Two passes, both above the snow line, both fought over whenever anybody is fighting here at all.`,

  'ulguroth-spiral': `A vast turning formation of land and shadow in the realm’s south-east, wound around a centre nobody has satisfactorily reached. Whether the Spiral is a landscape that grew this way or the visible edge of something else is not a question the plate engages with.

It is the southern half of Malerion’s dominion in everything but name, and the reason Greater Ulguroth has a soft interior and a hard shell. An army entering the Spiral is committing to a campaign measured in seasons: the arms are long, the rims are narrow, and there is no way to cut across.

Everything fought over in the south-east — Neff-Taal, the Choking Neck, Grydd Var, the Devouring Fogs — is fought over because it is a way in or a way through. There are not many of either.`,

  'bleak-forest': `A stand of dead shadow-forest on the Spiral’s northern arm, standing but not living. The timber is hard, dry and does not rot. It is the only building and burning material in the northern Spiral. Both sides in any war here need it and neither can burn it out of spite, because it does not burn well.`,

  'lone-cliffs': `A single line of cliff on the Spiral’s eastern arm, running for forty miles with one break in it. That break is the eastern arm’s only crossing. Everything that has happened in this quarter of the realm has happened within a day’s march of it.`,

  'galhain-spurn': `A spur of land on the Spiral’s inner turn, marked on the plate as corrupt — the cartographer’s word, and one used sparingly elsewhere on this map. Something got into the ground here and stayed. It is avoided by local custom and by every force that can afford to. Armies that cannot afford to cross it arrive on the far side smaller than they left.`,

  'the-devouring-fogs': `A belt of mist on the Spiral’s southern arm that does not behave like mist. Things that go in are not reliably there afterwards, and the Fogs have expanded twice within charted memory. They close the Spiral’s southern arm completely. The whole southern campaign in this part of the realm consists of getting around them, and the routes around them are the only ground worth holding.`,

  'grydd-var': `A stronghold at the southern end of the Spiral, watching the gap between the Devouring Fogs and the coast. Grydd Var is old, small and sited with total precision. It is the cork in the southern bottle. Nothing significant reaches the Spiral’s interior from the south while it holds.`,

  slaangoria: `A region on the Spiral’s southern rim where a Slaaneshi warhost settled in and made itself a country. What is there now is not what was there before, and it is not going away. It is a standing wound on Malerion’s southern border. His forces contain it rather than clear it, which is an admission the Lord of Shadow has never made out loud.`,

  'ruins-of-azyrsen': `The ruins of an Azyrite settlement on the Spiral’s southern shore — a foothold from the Realm of Heavens that did not hold. The stone is Sigmarite work and out of place on every horizon it sits on. Free City expeditions still land here, on the grounds that the harbour was chosen well the first time. The Spiral has so far disagreed each time.`,

  morladron: `Corsair’s Keep, a fortified anchorage on the Spiral’s southern coast, sheltered from the Devouring Fogs by the headland it stands on and from everything else by reputation. The Keep takes a share of what comes ashore and asks nothing further.

These are Idoneth waters — Ulgu’s lightless seas are the grimmest of all the Deepkin enclaves’ hunting grounds — and a harbour on this coast operates on terms the Deepkin allow rather than terms it sets. The Keep’s captains have made their peace with that. Everybody who has not is on the seabed.

It is the only defensible port on the Spiral’s southern shore. Any campaign in the south-east that expects to be resupplied by sea has to hold Corsair’s Keep or has to invent a new plan.`,

  // --- The Hypnoi Stretch and Liminus --------------------------------------
  'erstwhile-coast': `A shoreline on the eastern edge of the Ulguroth Spiral that the sea has left behind. The Erstwhile Coast is a beach with no water at the foot of it, and the water has not come back. It is the only flat ground between the Spiral and the Hypnoi Stretch, and every road east runs along it.`,

  'hypnoi-stretch': `A pale sea in the realm’s east, lying between Greater Ulguroth and the country around Liminus. The Hypnoi has a reputation among crews for what a long crossing of it does to the watch. It is the eastern realm’s main waterway and it cannot be avoided: the land route round the Stretch adds a month. Everything that reaches Liminus reaches it across this water.`,

  alaithru: `A region on the Hypnoi’s western shore, named in the old aelven form and drawn on the plate as something between a district and a watercourse. It is the ground the eastern road crosses to reach the Stretch. Nothing about it is remarkable except that there is no alternative to it.`,

  'dreams-end': `A site on the Hypnoi’s western shore where the mist that comes off the Stretch stops, abruptly and along a line. Where the line is, nothing is dreamt. Travellers camp here deliberately. An army that camps at Dream’s End sleeps properly for the first time since it entered the east, which is not a trivial advantage in this realm.`,

  godloss: `A town on the Hypnoi’s eastern shore, named for an absence rather than an event. Whatever was worshipped here is no longer answering, and the town has reorganised itself around that. It is the eastern shore’s only real port. Everything crossing the Stretch lands at Godloss, and the town charges for it.`,

  bladeweald: `A shadow-forest at the eastern edge of the charted realm, where the growth is hard-edged enough to cut. Harvesting Bladeweald timber is a trade and a hazard in equal measure. It is the last cover before the plate runs out. Forces operating at the realm’s eastern limit base themselves in it because there is nothing else to base in.`,

  'n-barrier-liminus': `The northern barrier ring around Liminus — a raised arc of ground and standing stones encircling the great gleam, built or grown to keep something in or out. It is a wall in a realm with few of them. An army that wants Liminus has to go through one of the barriers, and the north barrier is the one with roads.`,

  'clawing-forest': `A shadow-forest east of Liminus where the growth moves. Reports agree on the movement and disagree on everything else. It flanks the eastern approach to the gleam. Nobody advances through it; everybody worries about it while advancing past it.`,

  liminus: `Liminus, the Great Gleam — a broad pool of actual light in the Realm of Shadow, ringed by its two barriers and visible from the whole eastern quarter. In a realm defined by the absence of light, a permanent source of it is not a curiosity but a strategic fact.

What the Gleam is has not been settled. Ulgu’s scholars call it a wound, a leak from Hysh, or a thing that was here before the realm closed over it, and the barriers around it are old enough that whoever raised them is not part of the argument.

Every power in the east has wanted it. Nobody has held it long, which the locals attribute to the Gleam and Malerion’s court attributes to the locals. Either way, the ground around Liminus has changed hands more often than anywhere else in eastern Ulgu, and the barriers are the reason the fighting is always at the same handful of places.`,

  'aichiu-prowl': `A tract of broken forest east of Liminus, named for what hunts in it. The Prowl is not settled and is not crossed after dark. It covers the gleam’s eastern approach more effectively than the barrier does, and costs nothing to maintain.`,

  'trappers-bolt': `A camp south-east of Liminus, seasonal, fortified, and occupied by the people who work the Prowl and the Clawing Forest for pelts and stranger goods. It is the only inhabited place between Liminus and the eastern edge. Armies operating here hire its people as guides, because the alternative is having them as scouts for somebody else.`,

  's-barrier-liminus': `The southern barrier ring around the Great Gleam, the counterpart of the northern arc and in noticeably worse repair. That disrepair is the standing weakness in Liminus’s defences. Every serious attempt on the gleam in recorded memory has come from the south.`,

  'knifecut-inlet': `A narrow sea inlet south-east of Liminus, straight-sided and deep, running inland further than its width suggests it should. It is the only place on the eastern coast where ships can unload directly onto ground that leads towards the Gleam. That is why the south barrier is the weak one.`,

  'the-shackled-spines': `A chain of stone ridges in the far south-east, bound together by works that are neither natural nor recent. The name describes what was done to them rather than what they are. They are the eastern realm’s southern wall. Whatever the shackling was for, it has kept the Spines from being a route, and every army in the east has planned around that.`,

  'izendars-mark': `A site at the eastern edge of the plate, marked and attributed to a name that appears nowhere else on the map. It is a boundary stone in effect if not in intent: the point at which the surveyed realm stops and the cartographer put his pen down.`,

  'phantasmal-greyweald': `A forest of half-present trees at the realm’s south-eastern corner, which registers to the eye and not always to the hand. The Greyweald is charted because it is there; what it is made of is not agreed. Nothing supplies through it and nothing camps in it. It is the reason the south-eastern corner of Ulgu has never been contested.`,

  grimaice: `A town in the southern country below Liminus, cold even by the standards of a realm without a sun. Grimaice is a mining settlement working the ice-bound seams of the southern hills. It supplies the whole south-east with metal. There is no second source within two months’ haul, which is the only argument about Grimaice anyone has ever needed.`,

  'ithid-zar': `A stronghold in the far south-east, spired in the aelven manner and garrisoned by whoever is currently responsible for the frontier. Ithid Zar watches the southern approaches and has watched them for a very long time. It is the last fixed position before the Shackled Spines. Everything in the south-eastern quarter is held from here or is not held.`,

  mindlost: `A settlement east of Ithid Zar, on the road to the eastern edge, named for the condition of the people who arrive at it. It is a waystation and an infirmary of sorts. Armies returning from the far east pass through Mindlost, and the ones that stop there tend to stay longer than they planned.`,
};
